{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "O'Reilly-Shah et al. ([P-O2], [P-O3]) explicitly define plasticity as a nontrivial change to network parameters between timesteps that alters the network's input-output mapping, whereas S2 imposes no nontriviality constraint on b6: a completely static system with b6 w a x = w is a valid instance of S2."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "S2 restricts parameter updates to a deterministic, time-invariant rule b6 : B1 -> B2 -> X -> B1 depending only on current parameters, activity, and sensory input (ruling out stochastic weight drift or unmodeled exogenous signals unless encoded in B1 or X), updates B1 and B2 simultaneously from the previous step's values, and reads out solely from B2 via a6 : B2 -> Y rather than allowing direct dependence on B1 at readout."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "In S1.d16, interventions are deterministic, paired one-to-one with sensory inputs as List (X × P) (requiring a no-op element in P to model unperturbed steps), and applied strictly after each transition N.d2 σ xp.1; consequently no intervention can occur on the empty input list [] or prior to the first update step, while the final intervention acts immediately before readout N.a6."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "S1.d16 models only interventions on the internal state N.D1 while keeping the network's units, update rule N.a5, and readout N.a6 fixed; this matches the claim's focus on state-level interventions such as electrodes or TMS in [P-D51b], though it cannot express structural perturbations to network connectivity or architecture as mentioned in [P-U]."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The five definitions accurately formalize the author's claims: d15 implements a one-unit boolean latch retaining a trace of a past true input indefinitely, S2 with d3 and d4 models a general two-component parameter-and-activity system with evolving weights, and S1.d16 models step-by-step direct state interventions as an input-output function over List (X × P). The only divergences are minor idealizations and weakenings, notably omitting the nontriviality requirement on plasticity in S2 and fixing deterministic, step-ordered update conventions in S2 and S1.d16."
}
